--expect
whole=1 empty=0,0 small=3,0 mid=10 shares=11 clamp=4 rag=14
--expect-why
DERIVED BY HAND from `ropeslice.kin`, walking the descent for each range and
counting the byte offsets off the fixture. Nothing was read off a run.

THE FIXTURE, and every offset below is counted from it:

  four leaves of five bytes, one node over all four, SLICE_MIN = 4

      a          b           c            d
   [0 .. 5)  [5 .. 10)  [10 .. 15)  [15 .. 20)     total 20 bytes

`whole=1` -- `slice v 0 20`. `hi` is clamped to `n` (20), then `from == 0` and
`hi == n`, so kin returns `v` ITSELF. The check is identity, not width: a
version that rebuilt the tree would answer something 20 bytes long and print
0, and every `(subs s 0 (count s))` would copy the whole rope.

`empty=0,0` -- `slice 3 3` and `slice 7 2`. The guard is `from >= to`, so BOTH
the empty range and the INVERTED one answer the empty string, and both print 0
bytes rather than nil. `>` instead of `>=` sends 3..3 down the descent, where
`made` ends at 0 and the fold answers the empty string anyway -- so what this
field really pins is the inverted case, which under `>` would run the loop
with `hi < from` and take the `at < hi` arm never, and under a clamp of `from`
rather than an early return would produce something non-empty.

`small=3,0` -- `slice 5 8`, three bytes inside b. Width 3 < SLICE_MIN 4, so
kin copies: `s-copy-range v 5 8`, a fresh 3-byte leaf. Stated exactly, because
the field is weaker than the comment above it suggests: the descent would ALSO
produce a 3-byte leaf here (b's sub-range is itself under SLICE_MIN), and the
range is not child-aligned, so `small == b` could not have been 1 by any
route. What `3,0` pins is the width arithmetic (8 - 5, with `from` not
clamped and `to` not off by one) and that a short slice is never the child.

`mid=10 shares=11` IS THE SHARING CLAIM. `slice 5 15`: width 10, so no copy,
and `tree` is a rope so the descent runs. Walking the children with the
running offset `at`:

  i=0  a  at=0   at+w = 5 > from = 5 ?  NO   -- skipped, and `>` is why
  i=1  b  at=5   5+5 = 10 > 5 yes, at < hi;  klo = 0 (from is not past at),
                 khi = hi - at = 10, clamped to w = 5 -> klo=0, khi=w
                 => WHOLLY INSIDE: piece IS b, shared
  i=2  c  at=10  klo = 0, khi = 15-10 = 5 = w  => piece IS c, shared
                 at becomes 15 >= hi, so the loop breaks

two pieces, so `rope-from-roots` builds one node of 10 bytes whose kids are b
and c BY IDENTITY. `shares=11` is the whole point of the file -- a slice that
copied would print `00` and a 10-byte slice of a 509 KB rope would cost 10 KB
of copying instead of one node. The `at + w > from` test at i=0 is the strict
one: `>=` there would pull `a` in as a zero-width piece.

`clamp=4` -- `slice 16 99`. `to` is clamped to `n`, so the range is 16..20,
four bytes, all inside d. a, b and c are each skipped because `at + w > 16` is
false for them (5, 10, 15 all <= 16). For d: `at` = 15, `klo` = from - at =
16 - 15 = 1, `khi` = hi - at = 5, so the recursive call is `slice d 1 5` --
d is a LEAF, so that copies four bytes. One piece, so the fold answers it
unwrapped: 4. An unclamped `to` would index past the string; a `klo` computed
as `from` rather than `from - at` would take d's bytes 16..20, out of a
five-byte leaf.

`rag=14` -- `slice 3 17`, ragged at BOTH ends, which is the case where the two
boundary arithmetics are different from each other:

  i=0  a  at=0   klo = from - at = 3, khi = min(17-0, 5) = 5
                 klo != 0 -> slice a 3 5, width 2, under SLICE_MIN -> copy  2
  i=1  b  at=5   klo = 0, khi = min(12, 5) = 5 = w -> SHARED                5
  i=2  c  at=10  klo = 0, khi = min(7, 5) = 5 = w -> SHARED                 5
  i=3  d  at=15  klo = 0, khi = hi - at = 2, and 2 != w -> slice d 0 2      2
                 at becomes 20 >= hi, break

  2 + 5 + 5 + 2 = 14 = 17 - 3.

The last child is the one that catches a common slip: its `klo` is 0 and its
`khi` is 2, so the `(if (== klo 0) (if (== khi w) share ...))` must check BOTH
before sharing. Sharing on `klo == 0` alone hands back all five of d's bytes
and prints 17 rather than 14 -- a slice that is longer than the range asked
for, which no bounds check would catch because every byte of it is real.
